        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base mb-2">1. Taylor Cone-Jet Electrostatic Stress Equilibrium</h4>
        <p class="mb-3">
          When an electrical potential difference (12–28 kV) is applied between a conductive capillary emitter and a grounded substrate, induced surface charges accumulate at the pendant liquid meniscus. The outward Maxwell electrostatic stress (&tau;_e = &frac12; &epsilon;_0 E&sup2;) opposes the inward Laplace capillary pressure (&Delta;P = 2&gamma; / r).
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-slate-950 text-cyan-300 font-mono text-xs rounded-lg border border-slate-800 mb-2">
          τ_electric = ε_0 · E^2 / 2 = 2γ / r_meniscus
        </div>
        <p>
          At Sir Geoffrey Taylor’s critical semi-vertical cone angle (&theta; = 49.3&deg;), electrostatic stress precisely balances surface tension, pulling the meniscus into a steady conical geometry from which a stable sub-micron filament is continuously emitted.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Principle 2 -->
      <div class="p-5 bg-surface-soft rounded-xl border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base mb-2">2. Scaling Laws & Monodisperse Droplet Formation</h4>
        <p class="mb-3">
          Unlike pneumatic nozzles or ultrasonic horns that produce polydisperse sprays (PDI > 0.40), the steady cone-jet regime undergoes varicose axisymmetric Rayleigh-Weber breakup, yielding highly monodisperse micro-droplets scaling with the 1/6th power of flow rate:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-slate-950 text-emerald-300 font-mono text-xs rounded-lg border border-slate-800 mb-2">
          d_p = C_scaling · (ρ · Q_total^3 · ε_0 / (σ_e · γ))^(1/6)
        </div>
        <p>
          This micro-fluidic precision allows continuous production of uniform core-shell capsules (PDI &lt; 0.10) with exact wall thicknesses tuned by the volumetric flow ratio Q_core / Q_shell.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Principle 3 -->
      <div class="p-5 bg-surface-soft rounded-xl border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base mb-2">3. Non-Thermal Survival of Living Biotics & Enzymes</h4>
        <p class="mb-3">
          Traditional thermal spray drying exposes delicate probiotic cells (*Lactobacillus*, *Bifidobacterium*) and enzymes to temperatures exceeding 75–90°C, causing catastrophic protein denaturation, cell membrane rupture, and &gt;60% viability loss.
        </p>
        <p>
          EHD-CME is an entirely cold, isothermal process operated at 18–25°C. Electrostatic atomization requires zero heat input; the Coulombic repulsive forces split droplets at the molecular level, preserving 98.9% cell viability and active enzyme catalytic sites intact.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Principle 4 -->
      <div class="p-5 bg-surface-soft rounded-xl border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm sm:text-base mb-2">4. Zero-Oxidation Hermetic Core-Shell Shielding</h4>
        <p class="mb-3">
          Polyunsaturated fatty acids (EPA/DHA omega-3 marine and algal oils) possess sensitive methylene-interrupted double bonds that rapidly oxidize into cytotoxic lipid hydroperoxides under warm aerobic drying conditions.
        </p>
        <p>
